      "id": 1,
      "text": "Moab was named in 1880 after the biblical land east of the Jordan River.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        12
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Moab%2C_Utah",
      "category": "etymology",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "The town first appeared on the map when the Denver and Rio Grande Western Railroad crossed eastern Utah in 1883, bringing freight access.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        16
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Moab%2C_Utah",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "History Moab, founded by local guide Blaine Reniger, now publishes a comprehensive timeline book tracing the region from the paleo‑archaic era to modern tourism.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        3,
        10,
        11
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://historymoab.org/",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "Moab hosts the Canyonlands Natural History Association lecture series, where Reniger often speaks about the historic ties between Arches and Canyonlands National Parks.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        7,
        8
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://historymoab.org/",
      "category": "culture",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "The annual music festival that started in 1992 by New York musicians takes place each September, adding a modern cultural layer to the town’s heritage.",
